# NOTICE — what publishing this repository does and does not mean

This repository is **public and proprietary**. Those two words are not in tension and the
combination is deliberate, so it is stated here rather than left to be inferred from the
absence of a permissive licence.

## Public

Anyone can read this source, clone it, and watch it change. That was decided knowingly, on
two grounds and no others:

1. **Consumption without a credential.** The platform's other sub-systems install this
   package by pinning a commit — `pip install git+https://…@<40-character sha>`. Against a
   private repository every consumer's CI needs a long-lived read token, one per consuming
   repository, each of which has to be issued, stored and rotated. A public URL needs none.
2. **Branch protection.** The hosting provider refuses rulesets on private repositories on
   this plan and returns *"Upgrade … or make this repository public"*. The platform has
   carried *"no gate can technically block a merge"* as an accepted, disclosed residual
   since its first analysis. Publication is the first structural cure available for it, and
   the ruleset is enabled as part of the same act rather than left as an option.

**Publication is not reversible in any way that matters.** What has been public can have
been cloned before it is made private again. This was weighed and accepted.

## Proprietary

